message:
Now I see the vulnerability. In `fu_coswid_firmware_parse_entity`:

```c
} else if (tag_id == FU_COSWID_TAG_ROLE) {
        if (cbor_isa_uint(pairs[i].value)) {
                FuCoswidEntityRole role = cbor_get_uint8(pairs[i].value);
                entity->roles[entity_role_cnt++] = role;
        }
        for (guint j = 0; j < cbor_array_size(pairs[i].value); j++) {
                g_autoptr(cbor_item_t) value = cbor_array_get(pairs[i].value, j);
                FuCoswidEntityRole role = cbor_get_uint8(value);
                if (entity_role_cnt >= G_N_ELEMENTS(entity->roles)) {
                        g_set_error_literal(error,
                                            FWUPD_ERROR,
                                            FWUPD_ERROR_INVALID_DATA,
                                            "too many roles");
                        return FALSE;
                }
                entity->roles[entity_role_cnt++] = role;
        }
}
```

The issue is:
1. If `cbor_isa_uint(pairs[i].value)` is true, it writes `entity->roles[entity_role_cnt++]` WITHOUT checking bounds. `entity->roles` is an array of 6 elements (`FuCoswidEntityRole roles[6]`). If `entity_role_cnt` is already 6, this writes out of bounds.
2. Also, if `cbor_isa_uint` is true, the code still enters the `for` loop and calls `cbor_array_size(pairs[i].value)` on a non-array item, which could be problematic.

But wait - the description says "Heap-buffer-overflow READ 8". Let me look more carefully. The `roles` array is part of the `FuCoswidFirmwareEntity` struct which is heap-allocated. Writing to `roles[6]` would be a heap-buffer-overflow WRITE, not READ.

Let me look at the `cbor_get_uint8` function and how it's used. The READ 8 could be from `cbor_get_uint8` reading 8 bytes from a small buffer. Let me check the libcbor library:

<｜｜DSML｜｜=execute_bash>
<parameter=command>grep -rn "cbor_get_uint8\|cbor_get_uint16\|cbor_get_int" /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/fwupd/libfwupdplugin/fu-coswid-firmware.c | head -30</parameter>

